insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-an-earth-model-is', 1, 'A team keeps a folder holding a gridded top surface, a gridded base surface and a fault polygon. Why is that folder not an earth model?', '["The grids have not been contoured, so no map can be drawn from them and no thickness can be read off.","An earth model needs at least three surfaces to define two zones, and this folder holds only two surfaces and a polygon.","The files are in different formats and would have to be converted before anything could be computed.","The surfaces share no frame and nothing enforces their order down the hole, so nothing can be subtracted."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Without a shared frame the surfaces cannot be subtracted, and without an enforced order they can sit in an arrangement no rock could take. A folder tolerates both because it is only files, and the number of surfaces in it is not what makes the difference.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-an-earth-model-is', 2, 'What three parts make up the structural model built in this course?', '["A frame, a set of surfaces, and the zones between those surfaces.","A frame, a set of wells, and a fault polygon.","A set of contours, a legend and a scale bar.","A frame, a set of surfaces, and a set of rock properties at every node."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The container is the frame, the surfaces written onto it, and the zones between consecutive surfaces. Wells, fault polygons and node properties exist in the golden fixture but belong to the tiers above this one.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-an-earth-model-is', 3, 'Why is a negative zone thickness more dangerous than a surface that is plainly missing?', '["A negative thickness always means the depths were loaded in feet rather than in metres.","A missing surface can never be replaced, while a negative thickness can be ignored safely.","A negative thickness computes cleanly and enters a bulk volume as a quiet subtraction, so a wrong answer looks exactly like a right one.","A negative thickness stops the build until somebody corrects it."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A missing surface announces itself and halts the work, while a negative thickness gives a smaller volume that still looks like a plausible volume. Nothing in the output flags it, which is why the build has to report its own diagnostics.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-an-earth-model-is', 4, 'Which consumer of the model most directly requires the surfaces to be in the order the bit will meet them?', '["The choice of colour scale on a thickness map, since the deeper surfaces need the darker colours.","Well planning, since a prognosis that lists BaseB above TopB cannot be drilled to.","Archiving the project files at the end of the study, so that the surfaces load back in order.","Contouring a surface for a presentation figure, since the contours have to close in the right order."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A prognosis is read down the hole, so a stack in the wrong order is unusable to the drilling team. Volumetrics has the same requirement for a different reason, since out of order surfaces give negative thicknesses.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-an-earth-model-is', 5, 'In the golden model, zone B is the rock between which two surfaces?', '["TopA and TopB","TopA and BaseB","TopB and BaseB","BaseB and the base of the model frame"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Zone A is TopA to TopB and zone B is TopB to BaseB. The model frame is geometry and has no base surface of its own.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-an-earth-model-is', 6, 'What is the object handed from this course to the ReservoirCalc course?', '["The bulk rock volume of each zone, in m3, with the set of nodes it was computed over stated.","The picked horizons on their original source grids.","A contour map of each surface, as presented at the volumetrics meeting.","The model frame specification on its own."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Bulk rock volume is the last purely geometric quantity in the chain and the first input the property side needs, which is why the hand over sits there. It has to carry its units, its zone and the nodes it was computed over or the receiving side will use it wrongly.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-an-earth-model-is', 7, 'Which of these belongs to the ReservoirCalc course rather than to this one?', '["The thickness of zone A at a node.","The cell area of the model frame.","Hydrocarbon saturation at a node.","The bulk rock volume of zone A."]'::jsonb, 2, 'This course owns the container, which is the frame, the surfaces, the zones, the thicknesses and the bulk rock volume. Saturation sits on the property side of the hand over and is not taught here.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-an-earth-model-is', 8, 'Why are the geometric half and the property half of a volumetric calculation kept in separate hands?', '["Because the geometric half is arithmetic and too simple to review.","Because the two halves use different units of length and cannot be combined directly.","Because no software can compute both halves inside one project.","Because they change at different times, are owned by different specialists, and the geometric half can be checked exactly while the property half carries real uncertainty."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A new well moves the geometry while a revised fluid study moves the property side, so keeping them apart tells you which half moved a changed number. The geometric half can also be checked exactly, and mixing it into an uncertain calculation hides errors in the half that should have been exact.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-an-earth-model-is', 9, 'Zone A has a bulk rock volume of 45 x 10^6 m3. What does that number assert?', '["The volume of rock in zone A, as geometry alone.","The volume of rock in zone A that is of reservoir quality rather than shale.","The volume of rock in zone A at only the 320 nodes where zone B is also present.","The volume of rock in zone A and zone B taken together, which is 57.8 x 10^6 m3."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Bulk rock volume is thickness times area summed over the frame, and it makes no claim about what the rock contains. The two zones together do come to 57.8 x 10^6 m3, which is a different quantity from the zone A anchor of 45 x 10^6 m3.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-an-earth-model-is', 10, 'On which grid does the TopB source surface arrive?', '["40 x 32 nodes at 40 by 40 m from (900, 1900), the grid with the most nodes of the three","27 x 27 nodes at 60 by 45 m cells from (950, 1950)","30 x 25 nodes at 55 by 55 m from (880, 1880), on square cells","25 by 20 nodes at 50 m cells from (1000, 2000)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'TopB arrives on 27 x 27 nodes at 60 by 45 m from (950, 1950), which is the one source grid whose cells are not square. The last option is the model frame, and assuming a surface arrives already on the model frame is the classic beginner error this module exists to correct.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-an-earth-model-is', 11, 'What is true of the three source grids of the golden model?', '["Two of them match each other in cell size, and only the third has a different origin.","All three match the model frame in cell size but start from three different origins.","All three use square cells, in three different sizes.","No two of them agree with each other, and none of them matches the model frame."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The node counts, the cell sizes and the origins all differ, and TopB is not even on square cells at 60 by 45 m. None of the three matches the model frame of 25 by 20 nodes at 50 m from (1000, 2000).'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-an-earth-model-is', 12, 'The golden fixture also holds four wells, one of them with a 45 degree build, and a fault polygon. What is their status at this tier?', '["They are the control used to build the framework in module 2.","They belong to the higher tiers and are not examined here.","They replace the source surfaces once the model frame has been chosen.","They are errors in the fixture and should be deleted before the build."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Beginner slice builds the structural container from the three source surfaces alone. The wells are tied to that framework at the tier above, and the fault polygon is used at the tier above that.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-an-earth-model-is', 13, 'What is the fixed order of the four steps in the Beginner path?', '["Clamp depth down, resample onto one frame, integrate to volume, then difference into thicknesses.","Resample onto one frame, clamp depth down, difference into zone thicknesses, then integrate to bulk volume.","Difference into zone thicknesses, resample onto one frame, clamp depth down, then integrate to bulk volume.","Resample onto one frame, difference into zone thicknesses, clamp depth down, then integrate to bulk volume, since thickness is what the clamp acts on."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Clamping cannot come first, because surfaces on three different grids cannot be compared node against node. Differencing before the clamp would carry negative thicknesses into the volume, which is the error the ordering is designed to prevent.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-an-earth-model-is', 14, 'The clamped surface means on the model frame are 1539.5 m for TopA and 1575.5 m for TopB, each averaged over all 500 nodes. What free check does that give you?', '["Their sum should equal the zone A mean thickness over the same 500 nodes.","Their ratio should equal the number of nodes in the model frame.","Nothing, because surface means and thickness means are averaged over different sets of nodes.","Their difference of 36 m should equal the zone A mean thickness, and it does."]'::jsonb, 3, 'When both means are taken over the same set of nodes, the mean separation of two surfaces equals the mean thickness of the zone between them. The same check on zone B gives 1585.74 minus 1575.5, which is 10.24 m.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-an-earth-model-is', 15, 'A colleague reports that zone B has a mean thickness of 16 m. What has to be said alongside that number?', '["That it was averaged over only the 320 nodes where zone B is present, since over all 500 nodes the mean is 10.24 m.","Nothing, because a mean thickness over a zone is a single well defined number.","That it was measured in feet rather than in metres and needs converting first.","That it applies to zone A, which is present at all 500 nodes of the model frame."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Over all 500 nodes of the frame the zone B mean is 10.24 m, so the two values describe the same rock and differ only in the denominator, which moves the headline number by 56 percent. Quoting a mean with no statement of what it was averaged over is the reporting error this course drills hardest against.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-one-frame-many-grids', 1, 'Which of the three source surfaces does not arrive on square cells?', '["TopA, at 40 by 40 m","BaseB, at 55 by 55 m","TopB, at 60 by 45 m","All three are square, and only their sizes differ"]'::jsonb, 2, 'TopB arrives on 60 m cells across and 45 m up, so its resolution differs in the two directions. Rectangular cells are perfectly legal and happen whenever a surface is gridded to a survey geometry that is not square.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-one-frame-many-grids', 2, 'TopA arrives on 40 x 32 nodes at 40 m cells with its origin at (900, 1900). How far does it reach in x?', '["To 2460, because 39 intervals at 40 m is 1560 m.","To 2500, because 40 intervals at 40 m is 1600 m.","To 1560, which is the span of the grid.","To 2200, which is the eastern edge of the model frame."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Forty nodes have thirty nine intervals between them, so the extent is 39 times 40, which is 1560 m, running from 900 to 2460. The second option is the fence post error of multiplying the node count instead of the interval count, and the third confuses a span with a coordinate.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-one-frame-many-grids', 3, 'Why do the three surfaces of a real project arrive on three different grids?', '["Because somebody was careless when the surfaces were exported from the interpretation project.","Because different vintages, contractors and data densities led to different gridding choices.","Because gridding software cannot write two different surfaces onto one shared grid without merging them.","Because the grids were mismatched on purpose so that each surface could be identified by its geometry."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Each surface was gridded when and how it suited that surface, and choosing a coarser grid for a poorly controlled pick is the honest choice rather than a mistake. The framework build takes the surfaces as they arrive.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-one-frame-many-grids', 4, 'One report gives a mean depth for TopA computed over TopA''s own source grid, another gives a mean depth for TopB computed over TopB''s own source grid. Why can the two not be compared?', '["Because the two surfaces are at different depths, so their means were never going to agree.","Because one is quoted in metres below sea level and the other in metres below a rotary table.","Because a mean depth is never a meaningful summary of a gridded surface.","Because the two means were averaged over different sets of nodes covering different ground."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The two source grids have different node counts, cell sizes and origins, so a difference between their means mixes a real depth difference with a difference in coverage. This is the first appearance in the module of the rule that a mean means nothing until you say what it was averaged over.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-one-frame-many-grids', 5, 'What does the grid mismatch between the three source surfaces actually block?', '["Node by node subtraction, so there is no thickness, no zone and no volume.","Contouring, so no surface can be drawn until every grid has been matched to the model frame.","Loading the surfaces into the project at all until one grid is chosen.","Reporting the minimum and maximum depth of any one of the three surfaces."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Thickness is a subtraction taken node against node, and it needs the same node index to mean the same place on the ground on both surfaces. Each surface can still be contoured and summarised on its own grid, which is part of why the problem is easy to miss.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-one-frame-many-grids', 6, 'What is the most common way a project ends up with its model frame, and why is that poor practice?', '["It is computed as the average of the three source cell sizes, which hides how coarse the worst surface is.","It is set by the largest of the source grids, which makes the model cover ground that no surface controls and slows every later step.","It is inherited from whichever surface happened to be loaded first, when the frame sets the resolution, the extent and every denominator and so deserves a deliberate decision.","It is chosen by the drilling team, whose interest is the well path rather than the resolution of the model."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Adopting the first loaded surface''s grid hands the resolution, the extent, the cost and every denominator of the model to a contractor''s old default. The golden model''s frame of 25 by 20 nodes at 50 m from (1000, 2000) matches none of the three source grids, because it was decided.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-one-frame-many-grids', 7, 'The model frame uses 50 m cells. A channel about 30 m wide runs through the field. What can the model carry?', '["The channel in full, since interpolation between nodes fills in features of any width down to a metre.","Nothing reliable at that width, since features narrower than a few cells are smoothed away.","The channel, provided the surfaces are clamped after they have been resampled onto the frame.","The channel, provided the source grids it was picked on were finer than 50 m."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Resolution is fixed when the frame is chosen, a feature carried by one or two nodes is not a representation you should trust, and no later step recovers what the frame discarded. Believing a later step can restore that detail is the same error as believing a fine frame adds detail to a coarse source.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-one-frame-many-grids', 8, 'The model frame has its origin at (1000, 2000) and holds 25 by 20 nodes at 50 m cells. Where does its last node sit?', '["At (2200, 2950)","At (2250, 3000), counting 25 intervals across and 20 intervals up","At (1200, 950), which are the spans of the frame in x and in y","At (2460, 3140), which is the far corner of the TopA source grid"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Across, 25 nodes give 24 intervals, which is 1200 m, so x reaches 2200; up, 20 nodes give 19 intervals, which is 950 m, so y reaches 2950. The second option is the fence post error of multiplying the node count, the third quotes the spans as if they were coordinates, and the fourth is the corner of TopA''s source grid.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-one-frame-many-grids', 9, 'What is the rule for converting between node counts and distances on a grid?', '["Multiply the node count by the cell size to get the distance, and divide a distance by the cell size to get the node count.","Multiply the node count by the cell size and then add one cell of margin at each end of the grid.","Divide the node count by the cell size, and multiply a distance by the cell size to go back.","Subtract one from the node count then multiply by the cell size, and divide then add one to go back."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Nodes are fence posts and intervals are panels, so a run of 25 nodes has 24 intervals. Dropping the one does not raise an error, because the map still draws, it just covers less ground than intended and every number computed from it is quietly wrong.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-one-frame-many-grids', 10, 'How is a bulk rock volume built on the model frame in this course?', '["As the mean thickness times the total map area, without reference to the node count.","As the mean thickness times the number of intervals between nodes, which is 24 by 19, times the cell area.","As the mean thickness times the node count of 500, times the cell area of 2500 square metres, which gives 45,000,000 m3 for zone A at 36 m.","As the sum of the three surface means, times the cell area of 2500 square metres."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Each of the 500 nodes carries one cell area of 2500 square metres, so the node count is the accounting unit, and zone A at 36 m gives 45,000,000 m3. Using the interval count instead is the classic confusion of nodes with cells, and it produces an error of a few percent that a review will not catch because the answer still looks reasonable.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-one-frame-many-grids', 11, 'What happens at a single node of the model frame during resampling?', '["The value of the nearest source node is copied across unchanged, which is why a resample never invents a number.","The node''s world position is located in the source grid and interpolated from the four surrounding source nodes.","The mean of the whole source surface is written into the node and then adjusted by the local trend.","The node is left blank until the clamp fills it in from the surface above it."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Resampling is local, so each output node depends only on the four source nodes at the corners of the cell it falls in, and it is exact at coincidence, so a frame node landing on a source node returns that value unchanged. The clamp adjusts values that already exist and never creates one.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-one-frame-many-grids', 12, 'TopA arrives on 40 m cells and is written onto the 50 m model frame. What is the cost of that step?', '["Detail finer than the frame spacing is smoothed away, and a sharp crest can be flattened.","The surface gains detail, because a model frame carries more information than a source grid.","Nothing is lost, because interpolation between four source nodes is exact everywhere.","The surface becomes unusable, since a surface can only ever be resampled onto a finer grid than its own."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Writing a surface onto a coarser geometry than it came from cannot preserve what it carried at the scale of one or two of its own cells, and a crest can be flattened if the frame node lands on its flank. Interpolation is an averaging operation, so extremes soften and a resampled crest comes out slightly deeper than the source crest.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-one-frame-many-grids', 13, 'After resampling, what should the live node count of each surface be compared with, and what does a shortfall mean?', '["With the source grid''s node count, and a shortfall means the source grid was too coarse to fill the frame.","With the number of wells in the project, and a shortfall means the control is too sparse.","With the frame''s node count, and a shortfall means some frame nodes fell outside the source grid and must be left blank or declared as extrapolation.","With the clamp count for that surface, and a shortfall means two surfaces crossed."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A frame node outside the source grid has no four corners to interpolate between, so it must be left blank or extrapolated and declared, and the dangerous response is to extrapolate quietly and produce a full looking surface with invented ground around its rim. On the golden model every surface returns 500 live nodes out of 500.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-one-frame-many-grids', 14, 'What is the mean TopB depth on the model frame?', '["1539.5 m averaged over all 500 nodes of the model frame","1575.5 m averaged over all 500 nodes of the model frame","1585.74 m averaged over all 500 nodes of the model frame","1575.5 m averaged over TopB''s own 27 x 27 source grid"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The graded value is 1575.5 m and its denominator is the 500 nodes of the model frame. The other means quoted are TopA at 1539.5 m and BaseB at 1585.74 m, and a mean over TopB''s own source grid would cover different ground and is not a statement about the model.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-one-frame-many-grids', 15, 'Three surfaces now sit on one frame with all 500 nodes live on each. What has been achieved, and what has not?', '["Both, since surfaces that share a frame and carry a value at every node are ready to be differenced.","Neither, because a count of live nodes says nothing about either property.","Consistency but not comparability, so the surfaces still cannot be subtracted from each other.","Comparability but not consistency, since the surfaces can still cross."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Resampling treats each surface separately and never looks at the surface above or below, so a perfectly resampled stack can still describe an impossible geometry, and on this model the clamp has to fix 180 nodes on BaseB by moving it down onto TopB. Assuming that one shared frame also delivers a valid stack is the error module 3 exists to correct.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-depth-down-clamp', 1, 'Resampling has put TopA, TopB and BaseB onto the same 25 by 20 node model frame. What has that step guaranteed?', '["It has made the three surfaces comparable node for node, and it has not made them consistent.","It has made the three surfaces consistent, so no deeper surface can be shallower than the one above it.","It has removed any disagreement the three interpretations had before they were resampled.","It has guaranteed that every zone thickness computed from the surfaces will be positive."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Resampling is an interpolation done separately for each surface, so it makes the surfaces comparable at the same node without giving any of them an opinion about the others. Consistency is a separate step, enforced by the clamp.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-depth-down-clamp', 2, 'Depths in this model are TVDSS in metres, positive down. Which statement must hold at every node of a valid framework?', '["The depth of TopA must be greater than or equal to the depth of BaseB at every node.","The depth of TopA is at most that of TopB, which is at most that of BaseB.","The three surfaces must all carry different depths at the node.","The mean depths of the three surfaces must increase down the stack."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Positive down means a deeper surface carries a larger number, so the stack must not decrease as you read it downward. Validity is a node by node property, so a correct ordering of the mean depths of 1539.500000 m, 1575.500000 m and 1585.740000 m proves nothing about individual nodes.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-depth-down-clamp', 3, 'A framework has not been clamped and BaseB has come out above TopB at some nodes. Where does that show up first in the workflow?', '["As a null value in the BaseB surface grid at those nodes.","As a live node count below 500 on one of the surfaces.","As a negative thickness in the zone B thickness grid at those nodes.","As an error raised by the bulk volume calculation."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Thickness is the deeper surface minus the shallower one, so a crossed pair returns a negative number and negative thickness is the symptom. Nulls and live node counts are about data coverage rather than about stack order.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-depth-down-clamp', 4, 'Why is a node with negative thickness dangerous once it reaches a bulk volume calculation?', '["The calculation stops and reports the offending node index.","The negative value is stored as a null, so the node is dropped from the sum and from the mapped area.","The negative value is replaced by zero, which makes the total too large.","The negative value cancels part of the positive volume of other nodes, so the total is too small."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The sum accepts a negative contribution without complaint, so the answer comes out smaller than the truth and still looks entirely ordinary. Silent failure of this kind is the reason the stack has to be made valid deliberately before anything is built on it.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-depth-down-clamp', 5, 'At one node of this fixture the resampled BaseB is 1577.000 and the clamped BaseB is 1578.000, which matches TopB at that node. Which direction did the clamp move BaseB, and why?', '["It lifted the deeper surface up to meet the shallower one, because the clamp always favours the shallower pick.","It moved BaseB down onto TopB, because the depth value increased and depths are positive down.","It moved BaseB down onto TopB, because the depth value decreased and depths are positive up.","It moved both surfaces to the value halfway between them, which happened to be 1578.000."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The clamp sets an offending node to the running maximum depth, which increases the depth value, and with depths positive down a larger value is deeper. The lifting answer is the intuitive one and it is wrong, because the deeper surface is the one that moves and it moves down onto the surface above it.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-depth-down-clamp', 6, 'Why can the clamp count for TopA never be anything other than zero?', '["TopA is the shallowest surface, so there is nothing above it for it to break the ordering against.","TopA was resampled from the largest of the three source grids, at 40 x 32, so it carries the smallest interpolation error.","TopA has 500 live nodes, and a surface with a full frame of live nodes is never clamped.","TopA is the surface the interpreter picked most carefully, so it never disagrees with the others."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The clamp compares each surface against the running value carried down from the surfaces above it, and the first surface in the stack has nothing above it, so its value is always accepted. A nonzero count on the shallowest surface would point at a bug in the code rather than a problem in the data.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-depth-down-clamp', 7, 'When the clamp repairs a node, what value does it write into the offending surface?', '["The average of the two surfaces at that node.","A value a small distance below the surface above, so that the zone keeps a minimum thickness.","The value interpolated from the nearest nodes that did not need repair.","The depth of the surface above it, copied across unchanged."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The clamp assigns the running value, which is the depth of the surface above, so the two surfaces then hold exactly the same number. It does not average, smooth or impose a minimum thickness.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-depth-down-clamp', 8, 'What is the thickness of zone B at a node where the clamp has fixed BaseB?', '["A small positive value, because the clamp keeps the surfaces a short distance apart.","Null, because the node was repaired rather than measured.","Exactly zero, because BaseB now holds the same depth as TopB.","The negative value the surfaces had before the repair."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The repair makes the two bounding surfaces coincide, so the difference between them is exactly zero and the zone has pinched out at that node. The clamp turns an inverted zone, which cannot be interpreted, into a pinched out zone, which is ordinary geology.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-depth-down-clamp', 9, 'The house convention resolves a crossing by clamping down rather than by pushing the shallower surface up. Which reason supports that convention?', '["Pushing the shallower surface up would thicken the zone above it, adding rock nobody interpreted.","Pushing the shallower surface up would leave some zone thicknesses negative.","Clamping down is the only one of the two directions that produces a stack with no negative thickness.","Clamping down changes fewer nodes than pushing up would change."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Both directions produce a legal stack, so the choice is made on other grounds. Clamping down keeps the correction local to the zone that was inverted, leaves the better trusted shallow surfaces untouched, and never inflates a zone above the repair.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-depth-down-clamp', 10, 'What does the word monotonic describe in the depth-down monotonic clamp?', '["That every surface in the stack is smoothed the same way before the comparison.","That reading the depths down a single column after the clamp, the values never decrease.","That every zone in the model ends up with the same thickness at every node.","That the clamp makes one pass through the surfaces rather than iterating to convergence."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Monotonic means moving in one direction and never turning back, so after the clamp the depths down a column may stay equal but never go back up. The single pass is true of the implementation, and it is a separate property from monotonicity.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-depth-down-clamp', 11, 'How does the clamp treat a node that is null on one of the surfaces?', '["It fills the null from the surface above so that the column is complete.","It counts the null as a fixed node and reports it in the clamp count.","It stops the build, because the stack cannot be checked with a missing value.","It skips the null, leaves it null, and does not let it update the running value."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A null is never filled in and never advances the running value, so the next live surface below is compared against the last live surface above it. On this model that path is never taken, because all 500 nodes are live on all three surfaces.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-depth-down-clamp', 12, 'What clamp counts does this model report, in stack order from TopA to BaseB?', '["[180, 0, 0]","[0, 180, 0]","[0, 0, 180]","[0, 0, 320]"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Nothing was fixed on TopA, nothing on TopB, and 180 nodes were fixed on BaseB. The 320 in the last option is the count of nodes where zone B has positive thickness, which is a different quantity.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-depth-down-clamp', 13, 'The clamp fixed 180 of the 500 nodes on BaseB. What share of the model frame is that?', '["36 percent","16 percent","56 percent","10.24 percent"]'::jsonb, 0, '180 of 500 nodes is 36 percent of the frame, so over a third of the model needed geometric repair. Take care with the label when you write it up, because the 36 percent here and the 36 m mean thickness of zone A are unrelated quantities that share two digits.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-depth-down-clamp', 14, 'After clamping, TopB and BaseB both report a maximum depth of 1620.9999999999998 m, identical to the last digit. What explains that?', '["Both surfaces were resampled from the same source grid, so they carry identical interpolation rounding.","Where zone B has pinched out the clamp copied TopB''s depth into BaseB, so both hold one number.","The engine rounds every surface maximum to the same precision before reporting it.","The two surfaces were interpreted on the same seismic reflector across the whole model."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The repair writes TopB''s value into BaseB rather than computing a new one, so agreement to the last digit is what a copy looks like. The three source surfaces arrive on three different grids, so shared rounding is not available as an explanation.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-depth-down-clamp', 15, 'A colleague describes the clamp count of 180 as a nuisance from the gridding software. What is the better description?', '["It is a measure of how much smoothing the resampling applied to BaseB.","It is a warning that the model frame was chosen too coarse to resolve the surfaces near the pinch-out.","It measures how far the interpretations of TopB and BaseB contradicted each other, in nodes.","It is a count of nodes the engine could not compute, so those nodes carry no data."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The clamp repairs a node only where the surfaces handed to it disagreed about their order, so the count belongs to the input surfaces rather than to the software, and it would have been the same 180 whether or not anyone ran a repair. Treating it as a nuisance to be silenced throws away the only measurement of that disagreement.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-zones-and-thickness', 1, 'This model has three surfaces in the stack. How many zones does that give, and which surfaces bound them?', '["Three zones, one for each surface in the stack.","One zone, from TopA down to BaseB.","Two zones: zone A from TopA to TopB, and zone B from TopB to BaseB.","Two zones: zone A from TopA to BaseB, and zone B from TopB to BaseB."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Each neighbouring pair of surfaces bounds one zone, so K surfaces give K minus 1 zones. TopB does double duty as the base of zone A and the top of zone B, which is why the two zones share a boundary.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-zones-and-thickness', 2, 'How is the thickness of a zone computed at a node of the frame?', '["The depth of the base surface minus the depth of the top surface.","The depth of the top surface minus the depth of the base surface.","The absolute difference between the two surface depths, so the sign never matters.","The mean of the two surface depths at that node."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Depths are positive down, so the base carries the larger number and base minus top gives a positive thickness. Reversing the order returns a negative thickness at every node, which is a mistake that announces itself immediately.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-zones-and-thickness', 3, 'You have the mean depths of the clamped surfaces over all 500 nodes: TopA 1539.500000 m, TopB 1575.500000 m and BaseB 1585.740000 m. What mean thickness should zone B report?', '["36 m, from 1575.5 minus 1539.5.","16 m, because that is what a thin zone averages.","1585.740000 m, because that is what BaseB reports on the model frame.","10.24 m, from 1585.74 minus 1575.5."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The mean of a difference equals the difference of the means when both are taken over the same nodes, so zone B''s mean thickness over all 500 nodes is 1585.74 minus 1575.5, which is 10.24 m. The 36 m figure is zone A, whose bounding surfaces are TopA and TopB.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-zones-and-thickness', 4, 'The check that mean surface separation equals mean thickness has one condition attached. What is it?', '["The two surfaces must have been resampled from source grids of the same cell size.","The surface means and the thickness mean must be taken over the same population of nodes.","The clamp must have fixed no nodes on either of the two surfaces.","The zone must have positive thickness at every node of the frame."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The identity is linearity of averaging, and it holds only when both sides cover the same nodes. On a model where one surface has dead nodes the surface mean and the thickness mean run over different populations, so the check fails without anything being wrong.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-zones-and-thickness', 5, 'Which set of statistics describes zone A on this model?', '["Mean 36 m over all 500 nodes, maximum 42 m, minimum 30 m, positive at all 500 nodes.","Mean 36 m over all 500 nodes, maximum 42 m, minimum 0 m, positive at 320 nodes.","Mean 42 m over all 500 nodes, maximum 36 m, minimum 30 m, positive at all 500 nodes.","Mean 10.24 m over all 500 nodes, maximum 31 m, minimum 0 m, positive at all 500 nodes."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Zone A runs from 30 m to 42 m with a mean of 36 m over all 500 nodes and is present everywhere on the frame. The set with a minimum of 0 m and 320 positive nodes belongs to zone B.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-zones-and-thickness', 6, 'The engine reports zone A''s mean thickness as 36.00000000000001 and its minimum as 29.999999999999545. How should you read those values?', '["As evidence that the clamp altered zone A at a small number of nodes.","As a sign that the resampling has drifted and the surfaces should be rebuilt.","As values that should be rounded in the stored grid before anything else is computed from them.","As 36 m and 30 m, with trailing digits that are double precision residue."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Each thickness is a difference of two interpolated depths of the size these surfaces carry, so a residue survives in the last few digits. Report the rounded value of 36 m, and never edit the stored grid to tidy it, because comparisons downstream should carry a tolerance instead.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-zones-and-thickness', 7, 'A colleague states that zone A is thicker than zone B at every node of the model. What do the reported statistics say about that claim?', '["They confirm it, because zone A''s mean of 36 m is larger than zone B''s mean of 10.24 m.","They do not support it, because zone A''s minimum of 30 m is less than zone B''s maximum of 31 m.","They confirm it, because zone A is positive at all 500 nodes while zone B is positive at only 320.","They do not support it, because zone B has a minimum thickness of 0 m."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Comparing means or positive node counts says nothing about individual nodes. Zone B reaches 31 m at its thickest while zone A drops to 30 m at its thinnest, so the statistics leave room for places where zone B is the thicker of the two.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-zones-and-thickness', 8, 'Which set of statistics describes zone B on this model?', '["Mean 10.24 m over 320 nodes, maximum 31 m, minimum 0 m, positive at 500 nodes.","Mean 16 m over all 500 nodes, maximum 31 m, minimum 0 m, positive at 320 nodes.","Mean 10.24 m over all 500 nodes, maximum 31 m, minimum 0 m, positive at 320 nodes.","Mean 10.24 m over all 500 nodes, maximum 42 m, minimum 30 m, positive at 180 nodes."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Zone B has a mean thickness of 10.24 m over all 500 nodes of the frame, a maximum of 31 m, a minimum of 0 m, and positive thickness at 320 nodes. The 16 m figure is real, and it belongs to the 320 node population rather than to the frame.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-zones-and-thickness', 9, 'Zone B''s thickness grid is defined at all 500 nodes and 320 of them are positive. How many hold a zero, and what else in the framework carries the same number?', '["180 nodes hold a zero, and 180 is the count of nodes the clamp fixed on BaseB.","180 nodes hold a zero, and 180 is the count of nodes where TopB was clamped.","320 nodes hold a zero, and 320 is the count of nodes the clamp fixed on BaseB.","180 nodes hold a zero, and no other reported number matches it."]'::jsonb, 0, '500 minus 320 leaves 180 zeros, and those are the same nodes the clamp fixed on BaseB, because the repair copies TopB''s depth into BaseB and the difference between them is then exactly zero. The clamp count on TopB is 0.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-zones-and-thickness', 10, 'Zone B''s zero thickness nodes and the clamp count on BaseB should always agree. If they did not agree, what would that most likely mean?', '["The zone thickness was computed with the wrong cell area.","The model frame was chosen too coarse to resolve the pinch-out.","The clamp had been run twice over the same stack.","The thickness grid was built from the unclamped surfaces rather than the clamped ones."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The two counts measure the same nodes from two directions, so a mismatch points at the thickness grid having been differenced from the wrong version of the surfaces. That is a common slip in a workflow that keeps both the raw and the clamped grids in memory.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-zones-and-thickness', 11, 'Zone B is reported with a mean thickness of 10.24 m in one document and 16 m in another, both correct. What is the difference between them?', '["One was measured before the clamp was applied and the other after.","The 10.24 m is averaged over all 500 nodes and the 16 m over the 320 where the zone is present.","The 10.24 m is the vertical thickness of zone B and the 16 m is its true stratigraphic thickness.","The 16 m includes the pinched out nodes and the 10.24 m excludes them."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Both figures describe the same rock and differ only in the population they were averaged over. The last option reverses the two denominators, which is the error that follows from quoting a mean without stating what was averaged over.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-zones-and-thickness', 12, 'Why do the two forms of zone B''s mean thickness describe exactly the same body of rock?', '["Because the two means differ by less than the capstone tolerance of 0.05 m on that number.","Because the 180 excluded nodes are so few that they cannot move the answer.","Because 16 times 320 and 10.24 times 500 both give 5120, the total of the thicknesses.","Because the mean thickness of a zone does not depend on how the model frame was chosen."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The 180 nodes that one calculation includes and the other leaves out each hold a value of zero, so they add nothing to the total of the thicknesses while adding 180 to the count. Change the denominator, leave the numerator at 5120, and you get a different mean of the same rock.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-zones-and-thickness', 13, 'Every cell on this frame is 50 m by 50 m, so the cell area is 2500 square metres. What bulk rock volume does zone B have?', '["12,800,000 m3, whether you compute it as 10.24 x 500 x 2500 or as 16 x 320 x 2500.","12,800,000 m3 from the 500 node form, and a larger volume from the 320 node form.","45,000,000 m3, which is the graded bulk volume on this model.","5120 m3, which is the total of the zone B thickness values."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Volume depends on the mean and the node count only through their product, and that product is 5120 either way, so both routes give 12,800,000 m3. The 45,000,000 m3 figure is zone A''s bulk volume, and 5120 is a total of thicknesses in metres rather than a volume.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-zones-and-thickness', 14, 'Which statement of zone B''s mean thickness is the graded capstone value?', '["16 m, because it describes the zone where a well would actually find it.","10.24 m, with no population stated, because the capstone fixes the units rather than the population.","16 m over the 320 nodes where the zone is present, to a tolerance of 0.05 m.","10.24 m over all 500 nodes of the model frame, to a tolerance of 0.05 m."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The capstone fixes the frame as the population and grades 10.24 m to a tolerance of 0.05 m. That does not make 16 m wrong, and a mean quoted with no population attached is incomplete whichever figure it is.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-zones-and-thickness', 15, 'On the zone B thickness map, what does a node holding a value of zero mean?', '["That the node was masked because no control reached that ground, so nothing was computed there.","That TopB and BaseB sit at the same depth there, so the zone is absent.","That the thickness fell below the precision the grid can carry, so it was stored as zero.","That the node should be filled in before the map is delivered, so the contours flow across it."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A zero is a value computed at a live node and it says there is no rock between the two surfaces there. A null is the different claim that nothing was computed, which is about the coverage of the data, and on this model all 500 nodes are live on all three surfaces so no thickness node is null.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-bulk-rock-volume', 1, 'What is the bulk rock volume of a zone?', '["The gross volume of rock enclosed by the zone''s geometry, which is thickness integrated over area.","The volume of hydrocarbon the zone holds once it has been brought to surface conditions.","The pore volume of the zone, left after porosity has been applied to the rock.","The volume of net reservoir rock in the zone, left after the non-reservoir beds are removed."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Bulk rock volume is thickness integrated over area and nothing else. The other three options each apply a later step from the ReservoirCalc chain, namely a formation volume factor, a porosity and a net-to-gross ratio.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-bulk-rock-volume', 2, 'Which of these is already counted inside a zone''s bulk rock volume?', '["Only the pore space, on the grounds that the grains cannot hold anything.","Only the rock that sits above the fluid contact in that zone.","All of the rock in the zone, including the shale beds that are not reservoir.","Only the rock that passes the petrophysical cut-off for net reservoir."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Bulk rock volume is gross rock, so interbedded shale and other non-reservoir intervals are inside it. Removing them is what a net-to-gross ratio does, and that belongs to the ReservoirCalc course.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-bulk-rock-volume', 3, 'Why is bulk rock volume the natural object to hand from the framework workflow to the volumetrics workflow?', '["Because it is the smallest number the framework produces, which makes it the least likely of all the framework outputs to be wrong.","Because it is purely geometric, so it can be produced, reviewed and signed off before anyone agrees a fluid property.","Because it already contains the fluid contact, which leaves the volumetrics workflow less to do.","Because it is quoted in barrels, which is the unit the economics work needs from the geoscience side of the study."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The hand-off works because bulk rock volume depends on no fluid assumption at all, which makes it auditable and stable across every fluid case. It is the largest volume in the chain rather than the smallest, it holds no contact, and it is reported in m3.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-bulk-rock-volume', 4, 'The closed form check on bulk rock volume multiplies which three quantities?', '["Maximum thickness, node count and cell area.","Mean thickness, node count and cell size.","Mean thickness, the number of nodes with positive thickness, and the total area of the frame.","Mean thickness, node count and cell area."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Bulk volume is mean thickness times the number of nodes times the cell area, with thickness in m, the count as a plain count and the area in m2. Option 2 is the common slip of using the 50 m cell size in place of the 2500 m2 cell area.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-bulk-rock-volume', 5, 'Why is mean thickness times node count times cell area exact on this model rather than an approximation?', '["Because the three source surfaces were resampled onto a common frame, which removes any interpolation error from the thickness grid.","Because every node carries exactly one cell of the same area, so the cell area factors out of the sum and what remains is the node count times the mean.","Because zone A is present at all 500 nodes of the frame, so no node has to be skipped and every node in the sum carries a positive thickness value.","Because the clamp guarantees that no thickness anywhere in the grid is negative."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The identity holds because the frame is regular, so with a constant cell area the sum of thickness times area becomes that area times the sum of the thicknesses, and that sum is the node count times the mean. Zone A being present everywhere and the clamp both matter to the model, but neither is what makes the algebra exact.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-bulk-rock-volume', 6, 'Zone A has a mean thickness of 36 m over all 500 nodes of the frame, and the frame is at 50 m cells so every node carries 2500 m2. What is zone A''s bulk rock volume?', '["12,800,000 m3","57.8 x 10^6 m3","45,000,000 m3","It cannot be found from these three numbers, because a porosity is needed as well."]'::jsonb, 2, '36 x 500 x 2500 = 45,000,000 m3, which is the graded capstone value for this field. Option 0 is zone B''s volume, option 1 is both zones together, and option 3 confuses bulk rock volume with pore volume.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-bulk-rock-volume', 7, 'Zone B''s bulk rock volume can be computed as 10.24 x 500 x 2500 or as 16 x 320 x 2500, and both give 12,800,000 m3. Why do they agree?', '["Because the volume depends on the mean and the count only through their product, and 16 x 320 = 10.24 x 500 = 5120.","Because the engine rounds both results to the nearest 100,000 m3 before reporting them.","Because the 180 nodes without the zone are given the mean thickness of the nodes that have it.","Because the cell area of 2500 m2 is a common factor and cancels out of both calculations before they are compared."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Adding nodes of zero thickness to the denominator lowers the mean by exactly the factor by which it raises the count, so the product stays at 5120. The cell area does not cancel, it multiplies both results equally.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-bulk-rock-volume', 8, 'Zone B has positive thickness at 320 of the 500 nodes. Which pair of statements describes its mean thickness correctly?', '["10.24 m over the 320 nodes where the zone exists, or 16 m over all 500 nodes of the frame.","10.24 m and 16 m are independent measurements of the same grid, so only one of them can be right.","16 m over all 500 nodes of the frame, with 10.24 m being the value left after the clamp.","10.24 m over all 500 nodes of the frame, or 16 m over the 320 nodes where the zone is present."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Both figures describe the same rock and the same 12,800,000 m3, and they differ only in what was averaged over. Option 0 is the same pair with the denominators swapped, which is the most common way this gets misquoted.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-bulk-rock-volume', 9, 'A report states that zone B is 16 m thick and gives no node set. What is wrong with it?', '["Nothing is wrong, because 16 m is the value measured where the zone actually exists.","A mean without its denominator is not a result, and a reader will take it to apply across the whole model when it applies over 320 nodes.","The figure should have been the maximum thickness of 31 m, which is what a report needs.","The figure should have been converted to a bulk rock volume before it was reported, because a thickness on its own is never a deliverable."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Every mean thickness has to travel with the node set it was averaged over, because a reader with no denominator supplies one of their own. Between the two node sets the headline figure changes by 56 percent, and both figures are true.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-bulk-rock-volume', 10, 'How much gross rock do zone A and zone B hold between them on this model?', '["57.8 x 10^6 m3","45,000,000 m3","12,800,000 m3","It depends on which denominator is used for zone B''s mean thickness."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Zone A''s 45,000,000 m3 and zone B''s 12,800,000 m3 give 57.8 x 10^6 m3 together. Option 3 is the misconception the zone B lesson closes, since the volume is the same under either denominator.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-bulk-rock-volume', 11, 'A colleague asks you to produce the hydrocarbon volume in place from this framework. What is the correct response?', '["Apply a porosity and a saturation to the two thickness grids, then report the hydrocarbon volume in place beside the bulk volumes so the receiver has both figures to work from.","Read the hydrocarbon volume from the framework panel, which reports it beside the bulk volumes.","Hand over the bulk rock volumes with their frame and clamp counts, since the contact, net-to-gross, porosity, saturation and formation volume factor belong to the ReservoirCalc course.","Clip both zones at a contact depth of your own choosing and report the volume above it."]'::jsonb, 2, 'This course owns the container and stops at bulk rock volume. The framework panel shows the clamped surfaces, the two thickness grids and the framework statistics, and it computes no fluids at all.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-bulk-rock-volume', 12, 'In the chain that follows the hand-off, which step converts a volume at reservoir conditions into a volume at surface?', '["The fluid contact.","The net-to-gross ratio.","The hydrocarbon saturation.","The formation volume factor."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The formation volume factor is the last link in the chain and the step that finally produces a number in barrels or standard cubic metres. The contact clips the volume, net-to-gross removes non-reservoir rock, and saturation removes the water in the pores.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-bulk-rock-volume', 13, 'Why does a proportional error in the bulk rock volume survive all the way to the hydrocarbon volume?', '["Because the fluid parameters further down the chain are usually estimated from the bulk volume itself, which feeds the same error into the chain a second time.","Because every step after the bulk volume multiplies it by a fraction, so the proportion passes through unchanged and none of those steps re-examines the geometry.","Because the errors in porosity and in saturation always act in the same direction.","Because the formation volume factor is applied twice in most volumetric workflows."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The chain after the hand-off is a sequence of multiplications, so a container too large by a given fraction produces a hydrocarbon volume too large by the same fraction. That is why the bulk volume is the last place a structural error can be caught.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-bulk-rock-volume', 14, 'Which set of items should travel with a bulk rock volume when it is handed on?', '["The volume on its own, because any further detail belongs to the workflow receiving it and would only duplicate what that workflow already holds.","The volume and an estimate of the porosity, so that the receiver has something to check it against.","The volume, the model frame, the node count and cell area, the zone definition, the mean thickness with its denominator, and the clamp counts.","The volume and the three source grid specifications, which are the only inputs that matter."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A volume with no provenance cannot be audited or reconciled against the closed form check. A porosity is not this course''s to supply, and the source grids alone say nothing about the frame the volume was computed on.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-bulk-rock-volume', 15, 'In which situation does mean thickness times node count times cell area stop being exact?', '["Where the frame has variable cell sizes, so each node no longer carries the same area.","Where a zone pinches out, so that some nodes carry zero thickness and stop contributing anything to the sum.","Where the clamp has had to fix nodes on the deepest surface of the stack.","Where the two zone volumes are summed to give a total gross rock volume."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The cell area can only be taken out of the sum when it is the same at every node, so an irregular or corner point frame has to be accumulated cell by cell. A pinch-out and a clamp both leave the identity intact, as zone B shows.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-earth-modeling-workflow', 1, 'In the earth modelling workflow, which step produces no number of its own and yet fixes two of the three factors in every volume the model reports?', '["Resampling the three surfaces onto the frame.","Applying the depth-down monotonic clamp to the stack.","Differencing the surfaces into the two zone thickness grids.","Deciding the model frame."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The frame fixes the node count and the cell area, which are two of the three factors in mean thickness times node count times cell area. On this model that is 500 nodes carrying 2500 m2 each.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-earth-modeling-workflow', 2, 'What should a reviewer expect to hear about live nodes once the three surfaces have been resampled onto this model frame?', '["That the deepest surface has fewer live nodes than the others, because the clamp removes nodes.","That all 500 nodes are live on all three surfaces, so every node can produce a thickness.","That only the 320 nodes where zone B exists are live on the stack.","That the live node count differs by surface, according to which source grid each one arrived on."]'::jsonb, 1, 'All 500 nodes come up live on TopA, TopB and BaseB, so nothing has to be treated as missing anywhere. Where a surface is dead over part of the frame, the mean and the volume end up taken over different node sets and the closed form check will not reconcile.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-earth-modeling-workflow', 3, 'The clamp on this model reports counts of 0, 0 and 180. What do those numbers say?', '["That the clamp failed on 180 nodes, so the framework should be rebuilt from the source grids before anything at all is read from it.","That 180 nodes were removed from the frame, leaving 320 nodes to carry the model.","That nothing was fixed on TopA or TopB and 180 nodes were fixed on BaseB, which is the pinch-out of zone B expressed as a count.","That the clamp had to be run 180 times before the surface stack became valid."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The counts are reported per surface from shallow to deep, and 180 of 500 nodes is 36 percent of the model. The nodes are not removed, they carry zero zone B thickness and still count in the frame.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-earth-modeling-workflow', 4, 'When the clamp fixes a node on BaseB, which way does the surface move?', '["Down onto the surface above it, because the clamp sets the node to the running maximum depth, which increases the depth value.","Up onto the surface above it, because the clamp lifts the deeper surface until it meets the shallower one and the crossing between them disappears.","Either way, depending on which of the two surfaces lies further from the frame origin.","It does not move, because the clamp only records the offending node and leaves its value alone."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The clamp sets an offending node to the running maximum depth, so the deeper surface moves down onto the one above it. Reading the word clamp as a lift is the classic way this direction gets stated backwards.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-earth-modeling-workflow', 5, 'TopB and BaseB both have a maximum of 1620.9999999999998 m on the model frame. What does that shared maximum indicate?', '["That the resample drew both of those surfaces from the same source grid rather than from two.","That the two surfaces touch where zone B has closed, which is the clamp doing its job.","That the framework is invalid, because two surfaces may never share a value.","That BaseB was mapped from TopB rather than from data of its own."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A valid clamp allows a deeper surface to meet the one above it and never to sit shallower than it. The shared maximum is the pinch-out showing up in the surface statistics.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-earth-modeling-workflow', 6, 'Which subtraction confirms that the surface statistics and the thickness grids were computed consistently?', '["1620.9999999999998 minus 1585.74, which should return the zone B maximum where the two surfaces meet.","1579 minus 1500, which should return the zone A maximum of 42 m.","1575.5 minus 1539.5, which gives 36 for zone A, and 1585.74 minus 1575.5, which gives 10.24 for zone B.","1561 minus 1530, which should return the zone B mean of 10.24 m."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Mean surface separation equals mean thickness exactly on this model, so the difference of two surface means reproduces the zone mean between them. A mismatch says the two were computed over different node sets, or that one of them was built before the clamp.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-earth-modeling-workflow', 7, 'In the quality control pass, what is the rule about the minimum value of a zone thickness grid?', '["It must be positive everywhere, so zone B''s minimum of 0 m fails the check.","It must equal the mean thickness of the zone for the grid to be consistent.","It has no bearing on whether the framework is valid, because a minimum is a single node rather than a property of the whole grid.","Zero is permitted and negative is not, because a negative thickness means a surface pair is still out of order."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Zone A has a minimum of 30 m and zone B a minimum of 0 m, and both pass. A negative minimum would mean the clamp had not done its work.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-earth-modeling-workflow', 8, 'A framework reports a zone A bulk rock volume. Which check reconciles it without any software?', '["36 x 500 x 2500 = 45,000,000 m3, which is mean thickness times node count times cell area.","Comparing it against zone B''s 12,800,000 m3 and confirming the ratio between them.","Confirming that it is smaller than the two zone total of 57.8 x 10^6 m3.","Checking that it has been quoted to at least six significant figures, which is what the engine reports."]'::jsonb, 0, 'On a regular frame the closed form is an identity rather than an approximation, so a reported volume that does not reconcile is wrong in a way that can be traced. The usual causes are a cell area taken as a cell length, a unit change in the chain, and a mean taken over a different node set from the count.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-earth-modeling-workflow', 9, 'A submitted report gives zone B''s mean thickness with no node set attached. Which quality control check does it fail?', '["The check that all nodes are live on all three surfaces.","The check that the surfaces are in depth order everywhere after the clamp.","The check that the closed form volume reconciles with the reported volume.","The check that every mean is quoted with its denominator."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A mean thickness with no node set is not a result, because the difference between the two node sets changes the headline figure by 56 percent. The reader has no way of knowing which denominator was used.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-earth-modeling-workflow', 10, 'What does the framework panel show, and what does it not show?', '["It shows the clamped surfaces and the hydrocarbon volume in place, but not the thickness grids.","It shows the three source grids as they arrived on their own cell sizes and origins, but not the model frame they were resampled onto.","It shows the three clamped surfaces, the two thickness grids on the model frame and the framework statistics, and it computes no fluids.","It shows the two thickness grids and the water saturation on the model frame, but it does not report the clamp counts anywhere."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The panel is a view of the container. Fluids, contacts, saturation and hydrocarbon volumes are not in it, because they belong to the ReservoirCalc course.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-earth-modeling-workflow', 11, 'The capstone field for the number of BaseB nodes fixed by the clamp has a tolerance of 0. What does that mean for your entry?', '["That the field is graded to two decimal places, so 180.00 is what should be typed.","That the entry must be exactly 180, because a count of fixed nodes has no rounding in it.","That any entry within 1 of 180 is accepted, because a count cannot be read off exactly.","That the field is not graded at all, because a tolerance of 0 disables it."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The field is a count graded with a tolerance of 0, so 179 and 181 score nothing. The engine either fixed 180 nodes or it did not, and the panel reports that integer directly.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-earth-modeling-workflow', 12, 'The capstone field for zone A''s bulk rock volume carries the unit 10^6 m3 and a tolerance of 0.01. What should be entered?', '["45, because the field is in units of 10^6 m3 and the tolerance of 0.01 is in that same unit.","45,000,000, because that is the volume in m3 that the framework reports.","45,000,000, with the tolerance read as 0.01 m3, because tolerances are always in m3.","0.01, because that is the value the field is graded against."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The entry is 45 in the field''s own unit of 10^6 m3, and the tolerance is one hundredth of a million cubic metres rather than one hundredth of a cubic metre. Typing the full 45,000,000 into a field graded at 45 is the classic unit slip on this capstone.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-earth-modeling-workflow', 13, 'Which value does the capstone want for zone B''s mean thickness, and what is its tolerance?', '["16 m with a tolerance of 0.05, the mean over the 320 nodes where the zone is present.","10.24 m with a tolerance of 0.05, the mean over all 500 nodes of the frame.","10.24 m with a tolerance of 0.5, because thickness fields are graded loosely.","16 m with a tolerance of 0, because that value is exact."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The graded value is 10.24 m over all 500 nodes, and the 16 m over the 320 nodes misses by far more than the 0.05 allowed. Both figures describe the same rock, so reading the denominator the panel is using is what protects the field.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-earth-modeling-workflow', 14, 'The capstone asks for the mean TopB depth on the model frame, 1575.5 m with a tolerance of 0.1. Where is it read?', '["From TopB on its own 27 x 27 source grid, before any resampling has been done.","From the difference between the TopB mean and the TopA mean.","From the clamped TopB surface statistics on the model frame, after the resample.","From the maximum of TopB, which the clamp has set to 1620.9999999999998 m."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The field asks for the mean on the model frame, so it is read after the three surfaces have been resampled onto the 25 by 20 frame. Option 1 returns zone A''s mean thickness of 36 m instead.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-earth-modeling-workflow', 15, 'What does the Beginner tier hand on at the end of the workflow?', '["A hydrocarbon volume in place for each of the two zones.","The three source surfaces on their original grids, with a note saying which one is deepest.","A single mean thickness for the model, averaged across both zones together.","A frame, three clamped surfaces, a clamp count, two thickness grids and two bulk rock volumes."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Those are the ingredients a volumetric estimate needs, and they are where this course stops. The contact, net-to-gross, porosity, saturation and formation volume factor are all applied in the ReservoirCalc course.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'final', null, 1, 'TopA arrives on 40 x 32 nodes at 40 by 40 m from (900, 1900), TopB on 27 x 27 nodes at 60 by 45 m from (950, 1950), and BaseB on 30 x 25 nodes at 55 by 55 m from (880, 1880). The model frame is 25 by 20 nodes at 50 m from (1000, 2000). What does that tell you about the frame?', '["It was decided rather than inherited, since it matches none of the three source grids.","It was inherited from TopA, the finest of the three source grids, and rounded to a 50 m cell.","It was computed as the average of the three source cell sizes and their three origins.","It was inherited from BaseB, since the deepest surface in a stack sets the extent of the model."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A round origin at (1000, 2000) and a 50 m cell that belongs to none of the sources in node count, cell size or origin are the signature of a frame somebody chose. Assuming the frame comes from whichever surface was loaded first hands the resolution and extent of the model to a contractor''s old default.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'final', null, 2, 'One report gives the mean thickness of zone B as 10.24 m and another gives it as 16 m. What is the correct reading of that disagreement?', '["One of the two used the wrong pair of surfaces for zone B and should be discarded.","The difference is rounding, since the two values are within the graded tolerance of each other.","Both are correct, and they differ only in the nodes they were averaged over.","The difference shows that zone B was clamped in one report and left unclamped in the other."]'::jsonb, 2, '10.24 m is the mean over all 500 nodes of the frame and 16 m is the mean over only the 320 nodes where zone B is present, so both describe the same rock. The denominator moves the headline value by 56 percent, and neither number is usable without it.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'final', null, 3, 'Why do a mean of 16 m over 320 nodes and a mean of 10.24 m over 500 nodes produce the same bulk rock volume?', '["Because bulk rock volume depends on the surface means rather than on the thicknesses.","Because 16 x 320 and 10.24 x 500 both come to 5120.","Because the two calculations use different cell areas that cancel out the difference in the means.","Because the clamp removes the difference between the two before either volume is computed."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The volume depends on the total thickness summed over the frame, so with the same cell area of 2500 square metres both routes give 12,800,000 m3. Moving nodes of zero thickness in or out of the average changes the mean while leaving that sum untouched.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'final', null, 4, 'The model is rebuilt on a 25 m frame covering exactly the same ground. What should you expect?', '["Both the denominators of every mean and the bulk rock volume change substantially.","Nothing changes at all, because the geology under the frame did not move.","The bulk volume roughly doubles, because the frame now holds far more nodes at the same cell area.","Every mean now has a different denominator, while the bulk rock volume should barely move."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The node count and the cell area both change, and they change in opposite directions, so the ground area accounted for stays about the same. A volume that shifts materially when the frame is refined is a sign that something in the calculation is wrong.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'final', null, 5, 'Which operation makes a stack of surfaces comparable, and which makes it consistent?', '["Resampling makes them comparable by putting them on one frame, and the depth down clamp makes them consistent by enforcing the order of surfaces down the hole.","The clamp makes them comparable by aligning their values, and resampling then makes them consistent.","Contouring makes them comparable, and differencing them into thicknesses makes them consistent.","Resampling does both at once, and the clamp only reports statistics on the result."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Comparable means the same node index refers to the same ground on every surface, which is what resampling delivers. Consistent means no deeper surface is shallower than the one above it, which resampling never checks because it treats each surface on its own.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'final', null, 6, 'A surface that arrived on 55 m cells is resampled onto a 25 m frame. Which statement is true of the result?', '["It now resolves features it could not resolve before, because it holds many more nodes.","It is unusable, since a surface can only be resampled onto a grid coarser than the one it came from.","It looks smoother and more detailed, and it holds no more information than before.","It gains detail only at the nodes that coincide with nodes of the source grid."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Every new value is interpolated from the same coarse source values, so nothing is added that the source never measured. After a resample the node spacing tells you nothing about the resolution, and mistaking the two is why a coarse surface on a fine frame is so misleading.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'final', null, 7, 'On the golden model all 500 nodes come out live on all three surfaces after resampling. Why?', '["Because the clamp fills in any node that the resample left blank.","Because all three source grids cover the ground the frame occupies with room to spare.","Because a resample never produces a blank node under any circumstances.","Because the frame was built by padding the outline of the source grids outward by two cells on each side."]'::jsonb, 1, 'BaseB, for example, runs from 880 to 2475 in x against the frame''s 1000 to 2200, so every frame node falls inside a source cell and has four corners to interpolate between. A node outside a source grid has to be left blank or explicitly extrapolated, and the clamp adjusts values that exist rather than inventing them.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'final', null, 8, 'Which problem survives both the resample and the depth down clamp?', '["Three surfaces sitting on three different grids with different origins.","A deeper surface coming out shallower than the surface above it at some nodes.","TopB and BaseB sharing the same maximum depth value on the frame.","A horizon picked on the wrong seismic loop over part of the survey."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Resampling fixes the mismatched grids and the clamp fixes the crossing surfaces, but neither one has any way to know the pick was wrong, so the model faithfully renders the mistake. A shared maximum between TopB and BaseB is the clamp''s own output where zone B has pinched out, and not a problem at all.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'final', null, 9, 'Why is the graded mean TopB depth of 1575.5 m taken over the 500 nodes of the model frame rather than over TopB''s own 27 x 27 source grid?', '["Because the model frame defines the ground the model describes, so a mean over the source grid would cover different ground.","Because a 27 x 27 source grid holds too few nodes for an average to be stable.","Because the values on the source grid are less accurate than the resampled ones.","Because a mean can only be computed on a grid whose cells are square, and TopB''s are not."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A mean over TopB''s own source grid covers different ground, so it is not a statement about the model, and the frame is the denominator every model statistic is quoted against. Resampling rewrites values onto the common frame rather than improving their accuracy.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'final', null, 10, 'A framework build is finished. Which quantity leaves this course, and where does it go?', '["The clamp counts, which go to the petrophysicist for calibration against the logs.","The bulk rock volume of each zone, in m3 and with its denominator stated.","The model frame specification, which goes back to the contractor who supplied the source surfaces.","The three clamped surfaces, which go to the drilling team in place of a prognosis."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Bulk rock volume is the last purely geometric quantity and the first input the property side needs, so it is the interface with the ReservoirCalc course. The clamp counts and the surfaces are diagnostics and products of this course rather than the hand over.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'final', null, 11, 'The model frame runs from 1000 to 2200 in x, and BaseB''s source grid runs from 880 to 2475 in x. What would happen if the frame were extended west to 800?', '["Nothing, because the resampler extends any surface out to whatever frame it is given.","The new western nodes would fall outside BaseB''s source grid, so they would have nothing to interpolate from.","The whole of BaseB would go blank, since a model frame has to sit inside every source grid it reads.","The clamp would fill the new western nodes from TopB, since the clamp assigns a value wherever one is missing."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Resampling is local, so a node with no surrounding source cell has nothing to read and must be left blank or extrapolated and declared. The dangerous outcome is a quiet extrapolation that produces a full and confident looking surface with invented ground around its western rim.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'final', null, 12, 'The clamp reports 180 fixed nodes on BaseB. Which change could alter that count?', '["Reporting the zone B mean over the 320 nodes where it is present rather than over all 500 nodes.","Computing the bulk volume from the interval count of 24 by 19 rather than from the node count.","Rounding the surface means to two decimals before the thicknesses are differenced.","Choosing a different model frame, resampling differently, or supplying a different source grid, since the count comes from the clamp acting on the resampled surfaces."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The count is produced by the clamp acting on the resampled surfaces, so it depends on everything upstream of the clamp and on nothing downstream. Choices about denominators, volume arithmetic and rounding all happen after the clamp has run and cannot move its count.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'final', null, 13, 'Suppose TopA were redelivered on a 10 m grid representing the same interpretation. What changes in the model?', '["Very little, because the model frame at 50 m node spacing still governs what the model can carry.","The model resolution improves to 10 m everywhere, since the finest source sets the model resolution.","The model frame is rebuilt at 10 m automatically to match the new source grid.","Nothing can be built at all until all three source surfaces are redelivered at 10 m."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Resampling reads the fine source at the frame''s own node positions, so detail below the frame spacing is smoothed away on the way in. Getting finer resolution into the model means changing the frame, which is a decision with consequences for every mean and every node count in the build.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'final', null, 14, 'Which set of diagnostics should be read after every framework build?', '["The contour interval, the colour scale and the map projection used for the framework maps.","Only the final bulk rock volume, since every upstream step is already summarised in it.","The live node count and clamp count per surface, and the mean and range of each surface and thickness.","The number of source surfaces and the number of zones, which together confirm the framework built as intended."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Those numbers are how a build reports what it had to do, they have to be read with the nodes they were computed over, and each has a geological reading, such as 180 clamped nodes on BaseB standing for the pinch out of zone B. A single headline volume hides every one of the errors that produce a plausible looking wrong answer.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'final', null, 15, 'A framework build reports clamp counts of [0, 0, 180] on a 500 node frame and gives you nothing else. What can you already say about the zone B thickness grid?', '["That 180 of its nodes are null, because the clamp could not compute them.","That its mean thickness over the frame must be lower than zone A''s, whatever the surfaces look like.","That 180 of its nodes carry a small positive thickness left by the repair.","That 180 of its nodes hold exactly zero and the remaining 320 carry positive thickness."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Each repaired node sets BaseB to TopB''s depth, so the difference there is exactly zero, and 500 minus 180 leaves 320 positive nodes. Reading those repaired nodes as nulls is the classic confusion between an absence of rock and an absence of data.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'final', null, 16, 'Which pair of reported numbers gives you an internal consistency check on a framework, and what does a mismatch point to?', '["The clamp count on BaseB against the count of zero thickness nodes in zone B, with a mismatch pointing at the unclamped surfaces.","The live node count on BaseB against the number of zero thickness nodes in zone B, with a mismatch pointing at a model frame that is too coarse.","The clamp count on BaseB against the clamp count on TopB, with a mismatch pointing at surfaces in the wrong stack order.","The mean depth of BaseB against the mean thickness of zone B, with a mismatch pointing at a units error."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The two counts measure the same 180 nodes from opposite directions, so they must agree, and disagreement usually means the difference was taken against the raw surfaces rather than the clamped ones. Live node counts describe coverage, which is a separate question from stack order.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'final', null, 17, 'A partner document reports that zone B has a mean thickness of 16 m, with no population stated. What is the risk, and what should you do?', '["There is no real risk, because 16 m and 10.24 m agree within the 0.05 m tolerance the capstone allows.","The figure is wrong and should be replaced with 10.24 m, which is the only defensible mean for this zone.","A reader will assume it applies to the whole frame, so ask what was averaged over.","The figure must have excluded the clamped nodes in error, so the surfaces should be rebuilt before it is used."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The 16 m figure is correct over the 320 nodes where the zone is present, and it is incomplete without that phrase, because a reader who assumes the frame will overstate the zone. The right response to a mean with no denominator is a question rather than a correction.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'final', null, 18, 'Zone B has a mean thickness of 10.24 m over all 500 nodes, and 16 m over the 320 nodes where it is present. The cell area is 2500 square metres. What bulk rock volume follows from each route?', '["10.24 x 500 x 2500 gives 12,800,000 m3 and 16 x 320 x 2500 gives a larger volume, so the frame form must be used.","Both routes give 12,800,000 m3, because 16 x 320 and 10.24 x 500 both come to 5120.","Both routes give 5120 m3, because the cell area cancels out of the comparison.","10.24 x 500 x 2500 gives 12,800,000 m3 and the 320 node route cannot produce a volume at all."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Volume depends on the mean and the node count only through their product, and that product is 5120 either way, so both give 12,800,000 m3. 5120 is a total of thicknesses in metres, so quoting it in m3 is a units error.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'final', null, 19, 'Changing the denominator moves zone B''s mean thickness but leaves its bulk volume alone. Why?', '["Because the 180 nodes moved between the two populations carry zero thickness, so the count changes and the total does not.","Because bulk volume is computed from the surfaces rather than from the thickness grid.","Because the difference between the two means is small enough to disappear once it is multiplied by the 2500 square metre cell area.","Because the engine recomputes the cell area to compensate when the node count changes."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A mean has a denominator and a total does not, so adding nodes that contribute nothing to the total still lowers the mean. That is why a calculation which stays in totals is safe and why any average must travel with its population.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'final', null, 20, 'By how much does the headline figure for zone B''s mean thickness move when the denominator changes from all 500 nodes to the 320 nodes where the zone is present?', '["By 0.05 m, which is the tolerance the capstone allows on that number.","By 36 percent, the same share of the frame that the clamp repaired.","By 56 percent, with no change to the rock, the surfaces or the volume.","By 16 percent, which is the difference the two node counts make."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Moving from 10.24 m to 16 m changes the headline number by 56 percent, and nothing about the model changed while it happened. No tolerance in this workflow absorbs a difference of that size, which is why the population belongs in the same sentence as the mean.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'final', null, 21, 'The clamp fixed 0 nodes on TopB. Which independent statistic in the zone report agrees with that, and what do the two together tell you?', '["Zone B''s maximum thickness of 31 m, which together with the count says zone B was never repaired.","TopB''s live node count of 500, which together with the count says the surface covers the whole frame.","Zone A''s mean thickness of 36 m, which together with the count says zone A is thicker than zone B everywhere.","Zone A having positive thickness at all 500 nodes, which says zone A never needed repair."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A clamp count of 0 on TopB means TopB arrived below TopA at every node without help, and zone A being positive at all 500 nodes is the same fact measured by a different part of the workflow. A live node count describes coverage rather than stack order, and comparing means says nothing about individual nodes.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'final', null, 22, 'After clamping, BaseB has no value anywhere deeper than 1620.9999999999998 m, which is also TopB''s maximum. Which account of that is correct?', '["BaseB was lifted up to meet TopB at the deepest nodes, which caps it at TopB''s maximum.","At TopB''s deepest node zone B has pinched out and BaseB was moved down onto TopB.","The engine caps every surface at the maximum of the surface above it before reporting statistics.","BaseB was resampled from a coarser grid than TopB, so it cannot reach a deeper value."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The clamp sets an offending node to the running maximum depth, which increases the depth value and moves the deeper surface down onto the one above, and the value it writes is a copy rather than a new computation. The lifting account is the intuitive one and it is wrong.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'final', null, 23, 'A large clamp count can come from a genuine pinch-out or from two surfaces that were never reconciled. What should you do before accepting the model?', '["Map the fixed nodes and check that both surfaces share a datum, units and depth conversion.","Re-run the build with a finer model frame, since a real pinch-out will survive the change and an artefact will not.","Accept the model, because the clamp has made the stack valid and every downstream check will now pass.","Smooth the repaired surface so the pinch-out grades in gently, then compare the volumes before and after."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The count cannot tell the two stories apart, so the work is to look at where the fixed nodes are and to check the provenance of both surfaces. Smoothing the repair erases the pinch-out line, which is usually the most valuable feature on the map.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'final', null, 24, 'A colleague suppresses the clamp report so the build log stays clean, arguing that the framework is valid afterwards anyway. What is wrong with that?', '["The clamp cannot run correctly with its reporting turned off, so some crossed nodes will be left unrepaired.","The suppressed count would otherwise have been graded by the capstone, so the build cannot be marked.","Once the clamp has run every downstream check passes, so the count is the only evidence of the repair.","The count is the only way to recover the original BaseB depths, which are needed for the volume."]'::jsonb, 2, 'After the repair the thicknesses are positive, the volumes are positive and nothing complains, so a reader has no other way to learn that 180 of 500 nodes were repaired. That is why a framework should never be shipped with a clamp count that nobody can account for.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'final', null, 25, 'Zone A has a minimum thickness of 30 m and zone B has a maximum thickness of 31 m. What does that pair of numbers rule out?', '["That zone A has any node where its thickness falls below zone B''s mean of 10.24 m.","That zone B pinches out anywhere on the frame.","That zone A''s mean thickness of 36 m is larger than zone B''s mean of 10.24 m.","That zone A can be assumed thicker than zone B at every node of the model."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Zone B reaches 31 m at its thickest while zone A drops to 30 m at its thinnest, so the two ranges overlap and the everywhere claim is not supported. Comparing the two means is a statement about the frame as a whole and cannot settle what happens at a single node.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'final', null, 26, 'Suppose the model frame were extended further into ground where zone B is absent. Which of the two forms of zone B''s mean thickness would move?', '["The mean over the frame would fall, while the mean over the nodes where the zone is present would not move.","Both means would fall, because both are averages over the frame.","The mean over the nodes where the zone is present would fall, while the mean over the frame would not move.","Neither would move, because the rock in the model has not changed."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Extra nodes with zero thickness add to the frame denominator without adding to the total, so the frame mean falls, while the population of nodes where the zone exists is unchanged and so is its mean. Two means, two sensitivities, one zone, which is why the frame is a decision that belongs in the report.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'final', null, 27, 'How do a zero thickness node and a null node differ in what they do to a reported bulk volume?', '["A zero contributes no volume and shrinks the area the volume applies to, while a null contributes no volume and leaves the area unchanged.","Both contribute no volume and both leave the area the volume applies to unchanged.","Both contribute no volume, and the zero counts as ground you looked at and found empty, while the null shrinks the map area the volume applies to.","A null contributes its sentinel value to the sum, which is why nulls must be removed before any volume is computed."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A zero is a measurement of absent rock at a node you did map, so the area still counts as covered, while a null means the node was never computed and the volume therefore applies to a smaller area than the frame suggests. Quote the area alongside the volume so a reader can tell which case they are in.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'final', null, 28, 'Which sequence describes the beginner framework workflow in the correct order?', '["Difference the source surfaces to get thickness, resample the thickness grids onto the model frame, then clamp any negative values to zero.","Resample the surfaces onto one model frame, clamp the stack depth down, then difference neighbouring surfaces to get thickness.","Clamp the source surfaces on their own grids, resample the clamped surfaces onto the model frame, then difference them to get thickness.","Resample the surfaces onto one model frame, difference them to get thickness, then clamp any negative thicknesses to zero."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The clamp works on surfaces that already share a frame, because it compares them node by node, and thickness is differenced from the clamped surfaces afterwards. Clamping negative thicknesses instead of clamping the stack hides the repair, since it produces no per surface count for anyone to read.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'final', null, 29, 'The clamp fixed 180 nodes on BaseB, and zone B has zero thickness at 180 nodes. What is the relationship between the two sets?', '["They are different sets that happen to be the same size, because the clamp acts on surfaces and thickness is derived afterwards.","The 180 clamped nodes are a subset of the 320 nodes where zone B has positive thickness.","They are the same 180 nodes seen from two directions, because bringing BaseB onto TopB is what closes the zone there.","The clamped nodes are the 320 nodes where zone B is present, and the other 180 were left alone."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Where the clamp moves BaseB down onto TopB the separation between them is zero, which is exactly where zone B has pinched out. Quoting the clamp count and the zero thickness node count together is what makes the pinch-out visible in a report.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'final', null, 30, 'A candidate reports zone B''s mean thickness as 16 m and then computes the bulk volume as 16 x 500 x 2500. What is wrong?', '["The mean and the denominator no longer match, because 16 m is the mean over 320 nodes, so the volume comes out overstated.","Nothing is wrong, because the volume comes out the same under either denominator, as the zone B lesson shows.","The cell area should have been the 50 m cell size rather than 2500 m2 when the mean over 320 nodes is used.","The node count should have been 180, which is the number of nodes the clamp fixed."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The two valid calculations are 10.24 x 500 x 2500 and 16 x 320 x 2500, both giving 12,800,000 m3, because the mean and the count move together. Pairing the 320 node mean with the 500 node count breaks that pairing and inflates the answer.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'final', null, 31, 'Zone A''s maximum thickness is graded at 42 m with a tolerance of 0.05. Why does this field need no statement of a node set?', '["Because zone A is present at all 500 nodes of the frame, so every node set that could be chosen returns the same maximum.","Because a maximum is the largest single value in the grid rather than an average, so no denominator enters it.","Because the tolerance of 0.05 is wide enough to absorb any difference between node sets.","Because the maximum is read from the surface statistics rather than from the thickness grid."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Denominators matter only to means. Option 0 is true of zone A but it is not the reason, because a maximum would need no denominator even for a zone that pinches out.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'final', null, 32, 'A reviewer is handed one figure, a zone A bulk rock volume of 45,000,000 m3. What is the smallest set of extra facts that lets them check it?', '["The porosity and the hydrocarbon saturation that were used.","The three source grid specifications with their cell sizes and their origins, which show where each surface came from.","The maximum and the minimum thickness of the zone.","The mean thickness with the node set it was averaged over, the node count, and the cell area."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Those three are the factors in the closed form, so the reviewer can run 36 x 500 x 2500 = 45,000,000 m3 in their head. Fluid properties are not inputs to a bulk volume, and the source grids say nothing about the frame the volume was computed on.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'final', null, 33, 'A thickness grid was built from the resampled surfaces before the clamp was applied, while the surface statistics were taken after it. Which check catches the mismatch?', '["The check that all 500 nodes are live on all three surfaces.","The check that mean surface separation equals mean thickness.","The check that the clamp count is reported rather than silenced.","The check that every mean is quoted with its denominator."]'::jsonb, 1, 'On this model 1575.5 minus 1539.5 gives 36 and 1585.74 minus 1575.5 gives 10.24, matching the two mean thicknesses exactly. An unclamped zone B would carry negative thicknesses where the surfaces crossed, so its mean would no longer be 10.24 m and the separation would not reproduce it.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'final', null, 34, 'Which statement correctly divides the work between this course and the ReservoirCalc course?', '["This course produces the bulk volume and the porosity that goes with it, and ReservoirCalc supplies the contact, the saturation and the formation volume factor that follow.","This course produces the hydrocarbon volume in place, and ReservoirCalc converts it to surface volumes with the formation volume factor.","This course produces the container and stops at bulk rock volume, and the contact, net-to-gross, porosity, saturation and formation volume factor all belong to ReservoirCalc.","Both courses compute the bulk volume independently from the same surfaces, and the two results are reconciled against each other at the end of the study."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The bulk rock volume is the hand-off object between the two courses. None of the five downstream quantities is a property of the geometry, so none of them can be derived from a surface stack.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'final', null, 35, 'Why is it useful in practice to keep the container separate from the fluid parameters rather than computing everything in one workflow?', '["Because the two change on different clocks, and a fluid revision would otherwise drag the container through a rebuild that can shift the frame, the clamp or the zone definition.","Because the container is the harder of the two to compute, so it should always be finished and signed off before any of the fluid work is allowed to start.","Because a bulk rock volume is only valid for one fluid case at a time, so a new case means the container has to be built again.","Because the fluid parameters are measured more accurately than the geometry, so mixing the two would drag the better numbers down."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Structural inputs are revised when a surface is remapped, while fluid parameters are revised far more often. The same 45,000,000 m3 supports every fluid case without being rebuilt, which is the opposite of what option 2 claims.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'final', null, 36, 'TopA arrives on a 40 x 32 grid, TopB on a 27 x 27 grid and BaseB on a 30 x 25 grid, each with its own cell size and origin. Why can the framework not just be built on one of those grids?', '["Because a source grid always carries fewer nodes than a model frame, so a framework built on one would lose detail.","Because the clamp can only be applied to a grid at 50 m cells.","Because thickness can only be computed on the grid that the deepest surface arrived on, and that is the grid the base of the model was mapped upon.","Because none of the three matches either of the others, so no pair of surfaces could be differenced node by node until all three sit on one common frame."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Thickness is a node by node difference, which requires the surfaces to share nodes. That is why the model frame is a decision taken up front and why resampling is the first thing a framework build has to do.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'final', null, 37, 'How should a clamp count of 180 on BaseB be treated in a report?', '["As a defect, to be removed by remapping BaseB and rerunning the framework until the count has fallen to zero on every surface.","As information, because 180 of 500 nodes is 36 percent of the model and it is the pinch-out of zone B expressed as a number.","As an internal diagnostic that belongs in the engine log rather than in the report.","As a count of nodes dropped from the frame, leaving 320 nodes to work with."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Suppressing the count hides the most important structural fact about zone B. The nodes are not dropped, they carry zero zone B thickness and still count in the frame and in the volume.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'final', null, 38, 'Zone A holds 45,000,000 m3 and zone B holds 12,800,000 m3, giving 57.8 x 10^6 m3 together. Why does that total not inherit the denominator problem that zone B''s mean thickness has?', '["Because zone B''s contribution is small enough that the difference does not matter.","Because the total is quoted in 10^6 m3, which absorbs the difference between the two node sets.","Because volumes add, while a mean depends on what was averaged over.","Because the total is computed over the 320 nodes where both zones are present."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The total is a sum of two volumes rather than an average of anything, and each of those volumes is the same under either denominator. Zone A is present at all 500 nodes and zone B at 320, so option 3 misstates the geometry as well.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'final', null, 39, 'Which of the six graded capstone fields admits no tolerance at all?', '["The number of BaseB nodes fixed by the clamp.","Zone A''s mean thickness over the frame.","Zone A''s bulk rock volume.","The mean TopB depth on the model frame."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The clamp count is graded with a tolerance of 0, so the entry must be exactly 180. The other three carry tolerances of 0.05, 0.01 and 0.1 in their own units.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'final', null, 40, 'Zone A''s bulk rock volume is graded with a tolerance of 0.01. In what unit is that tolerance?', '["In m3, so the entry has to land within 0.01 m3 of the reported 45,000,000 m3 for the field to score.","In m, because the field is derived from a mean thickness.","It is a fraction, so it means one percent of the graded value.","In 10^6 m3, the unit of the field itself, so it is one hundredth of a million cubic metres."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A tolerance is always expressed in the unit of the field it belongs to, and this field''s unit is 10^6 m3 with an expected value of 45. Reading it as m3 is what leads candidates to type the full 45,000,000 into the field.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'final', null, 41, 'Why is zone A''s mean thickness unambiguous while zone B''s has to be stated with a node set?', '["Because zone A''s thickness grid was computed before the clamp and zone B''s after it.","Because zone A''s mean is graded in the capstone and zone B''s is not.","Because zone A has positive thickness at all 500 nodes, so only one node set is available, while zone B is present at 320 of the 500.","Because zone A is the thicker of the two zones, so a change of denominator is too small a shift to show up in its mean thickness."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A denominator only becomes a choice once some nodes carry zero thickness. Both zone means are graded, at 36 m and at 10.24 m, so option 1 is wrong on the facts as well.'),
  ('earthmodel', 'beginner', 'final', null, 42, 'Which of these is a complete statement of zone B for a report?', '["Zone B has a mean thickness of 16 m and a bulk rock volume of 12,800,000 m3, with a maximum thickness of 31 m and a minimum of 0 m across the model frame.","Zone B holds 12,800,000 m3 of gross rock, with a mean thickness of 10.24 m over all 500 nodes or 16 m over the 320 nodes where it is present, and it is absent at the 180 nodes the clamp fixed.","Zone B has a maximum thickness of 31 m and a minimum of 0 m over the model frame, and those two values bracket the zone.","Zone B holds 12,800,000 m3 of gross rock, which is the same figure under either denominator and is therefore the only number a reader actually needs."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The volume is the deliverable, the two means with their node sets are the context, and the pinch-out has to be stated because a zone absent over 180 nodes behaves differently downstream. Option 0 quotes a mean with no denominator, which is the most common failure of all.');
